Reconstruct this block from an observable mark on a rotating object. Explain orientation modulo a full turn, unwrapped angular displacement, angular velocity and acceleration; derive tangential and radial acceleration in a fixed frame. State what rigid-body and fixed-axis assumptions made the calculation possible. Use the explicit cross-product components to connect the earlier planar moment with three-dimensional angular momentum.

For a particle and a closed constant-mass system, derive the angular-momentum balance and the required internal-torque cancellation. Change the origin and account for the altered angular momentum and torque. Derive the moving-origin transport term with absolute momenta, then explain why the center of mass is a special case. A moment about an arbitrary moving contact point needs a derivation rather than an appeal to a memorized formula.

Build axial inertia from masses and a simple integral. Prove the rod midpoint approximation, parallel-axis formula and nonnegative tensor quadratic form. Distinguish mass inertia from area moment and recognize the singular ideal line-mass case. Separate kinetic energy into center translation and central rotation. Explain when L is parallel to ω, when only the axial balance reduces to Iα, and what additional derivative term occurs in body components.

Solve the rolling example from translation, moment balance and initial/contact constraints. Explain how the required static force changes sign and why a formally solved acceleration may be physically inadmissible. Reconcile zero ideal contact power with nonzero force and separate translational/rotational energy changes. For the rotor pair, derive conservation, the energy minimum, partial exchange and the overshoot counterexample; state why a constitutive time law is extra information.

The original investigation and bounded published washer calculation provide executable evidence. Their reproducibility does not establish a measured property or claim that the larger source chapters, exercises or independent research assignments have been completed. Present the parameter ambiguity, a discriminating observation and a revised conclusion if assumptions fail. Retain source edition, actual inspected passage and reuse boundaries.

**Completion evidence for this block:** a connected written explanation and independently reconstructed accounts; correctly stated axes, signs, units, force/torque boundaries and assumptions; the supplied program checks plus a substantive extension with a justified convergence or error argument; a critique and revision that separates mathematical consequences from empirical evidence. Completing a file count is insufficient. Return to any shared lesson needed to repair a gap.

